18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1045 "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064411 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0dc1fb7b-6336-57cd-b507-8bd6ddfc25fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064952 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064952 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064952 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064952 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064952 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6fafd98-c438-5a64-a87a-7a2d0a58fd7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064952 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2\\,\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}=4\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $2x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065076 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065076 #edb/ref 1061 641164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065076 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3e15f90-13b8-5437-b44e-fcdec07ad48c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065076 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065077 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065077 #edb/ref 1061 641165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065077 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5880b8e-1ebf-531e-9458-f3312a599e31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065077 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065078 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065078 #edb/ref 1061 641168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065078 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6a53452-9624-525f-9b54-d025309e71da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065078 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065079 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065079 #edb/ref 1061 641174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065079 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5a617a2-9f5d-550f-a473-195c08b4798c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065079 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065124 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065124 #edb/ref 1061 641171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065124 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c6cd5c4-2775-585d-9234-d995757ade98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065124 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065182 #edb/ref 1061 641177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065182 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b420ebdd-a5d6-5ce1-8cde-23990b2968f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065182 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065381 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065381 #edb/ref 1065 4800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065381 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02539765-4345-5409-87f9-9901a59b7ebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065381 #edb/ref 1067 "Summary of Key Results" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065433 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral, apart from the coefficient of $4$ in the denominator, resembles the basic integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nIn fact, they look almost the same, but instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = (2x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can often use this trick whenever we see an integral that closely resembles an integral for an inverse trigonometric function.\n\n**Note:** As always, after solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065433 #edb/ref 1065 863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "53af5e98-4580-5243-a97c-de9e0d5f8fab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065433 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247570 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac